p the vapor layer from  forming and keeps the liquid in contact  with the hot material but this only  works up to a point so far the highest  like the first point  is around 500 degree no one has been  able to stop the leidenfrost effect  happening at extremely high temperatures  until now  zuankai and his team have developed what  they call a structured thermal armor  the new design includes a special  membrane embedded in the channels which  wicks the water down into the grooves  the membrane can suck the water  very efficiently so when the water  contacts the membrane the water is  absorbed  and then spread on the surface and then  become evaporated the combination of the  texture and the absorbent membrane stops  the droplets from bouncing off and helps  the liquid evaporate so it can  efficiently cool down the hot surface  so how hot can this new technique go  actually that limit for uh there's no  limit because on our armor we couldn't  see the lighting froze in fact  during testing their structured thermal  armor reached 1200 degrees celsius  without any bouncing droplets but at  that point the steel it was made from  started to melt  zwonkai thinks with different materials  it could get even hotter  and the flexible armor could be used in  a range of cooling applications for  example in nuclear power plant right we  want to remove the heat at high  temperature  and also in turbine the engine right or  any aircraft  the current approach is not that  efficient  so think about your one day  we can replace  the current material without our armor  and then you can just spread the water  on armor and suddenly  the temperature will drop
 

